
##  Hardware Design: Rev A Schematic

The schematic captures a highly efficient synchronous buck converter stepping down a nominal 12V automotive/battery input rail to a regulated 5.06V auxiliary output rail capable of supplying up to 3A.

###  PCB Layout: Pre-Routing Floorplan
Prior to drawing copper tracks, components were grouped into logical functional "neighborhoods" to optimize electrical performance and signal integrity. The placement follows a strict left-to-right power flow architecture, prioritizing an ultra-tight input "hot loop" ($C_5$ to $V_{\text{IN}}/PGND$) to minimize high-frequency switching noise. Sensitive analog control elements, such as the feedback divider network ($R_{\text{top}}$ and $R_2$), are physically isolated on the opposite side of the controller IC, using the chip body as a natural shield against the magnetic fields generated by the power inductor ($L_1$).
